TRACE_ASSISTANT line=26 text=The target is the GPA-to-letter-grade mapper. The critical proof issue is the supplied semantics’ treatment of floats: concrete arithmetic/comparisons may be opaque or backend-only, so I’m checking the float and list/function rules before choosing an implementation and claim shape that the symbolic backend can actually close.
TRACE_ASSISTANT line=80 text=I’m using a proof harness that expands into the exact translated function body, then proves symbolic one-element classifications under each contract region. This avoids pretending that the Haskell prover can numerically evaluate IEEE comparisons; instead, each claim states the corresponding comparison-path assumptions explicitly, while LLVM `krun` will validate the actual concrete thresholds and the prompt example.
TRACE_ASSISTANT line=107 text=The first proof exposed an actual backend limitation documented in the supplied float module: Haskell has no `FLOAT.eq` hook. I’m adding a proof-only opaque equality atom at the exact `grade == 4.0` comparison site; it preserves the branch decision symbolically and leaves the LLVM execution semantics untouched.
TRACE_ASSISTANT line=238 text=The concrete suite now passes under LLVM, including the prompt example and every threshold boundary. I’m also testing a circular loop invariant so the proof covers arbitrary-length float lists, not only isolated grading branches.
TRACE_ASSISTANT line=295 text=The arbitrary-list invariant now closes with `#Top`. I’m strengthening it to cover mixed integer/float GPA lists (as in the prompt example) by normalizing each GPA with Python’s supported `float()` builtin before the decision tree; this is semantics-preserving for the task’s GPA domain and makes the symbolic list element type uniform.
TRACE_ASSISTANT line=352 text=The mixed numeric loop proof also closed. I’m now checking whether K can reuse that invariant to close a whole-function theorem (allocation, frame setup, arbitrary list, return) in the same proof run; this is the final strengthening step and is taking longer than the standalone invariant.
